ue\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle mean|= (2(2) + 5(3) + 8(2) + 11(1))/(8); = \\frac{46}{8}; = 5.75.\n$$\n- For the table\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nthe mean of the data set is\n$$\n\\displaystyle mean|= (2(2) + 5(2) + 8(1) + 11(3))/(8); = \\frac{55}{8}; \\approx 6.88.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the frequency table whose data set has the greatest mean is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [:db/cas 17592186228762 :question/problem "A museum surveyed visitors about four exhibits, asking each to choose the one they enjoyed most. Each response was submitted either at an on-site kiosk or via an email link. The table below shows the distribution of preferences, in percent, by response method. The total number of email responses was four times the number of kiosk responses.\n\n|  | Kiosk | Email |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Sculpture | $18\\%$ | $22\\%$ |\n| Painting | $37\\%$ | $28\\%$ |\n| Science | $22\\%$ | $30\\%$ |\n| History | $23\\%$ | $20\\%$ |\n| **Total** | **$100\\%$** | **$100\\%$** |\n\nIf a visitor who preferred the sculpture exhibit is selected at random, what is the probability that the visitor responded via email?" "A museum surveyed visitors about four exhibits, asking each to choose the one they enjoyed most. Each response was submitted either at an on-site kiosk or via an email link. The table below shows the distribution of preferences, in percent, by response method. The total number of email responses was four times the number of kiosk responses.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Kiosk</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Email</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Sculpture</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18\\%$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Painting</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$37\\%$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Science</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22\\%$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">History</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23\\%$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20\\%$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$100\\%$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$100\\%$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf a visitor who preferred the sculpture exhibit is selected at random, what is the probability that the visitor responded via email?"] [:db/cas 17592186228764 :question/problem "An electronics shop records whether a buyer’s main purchase was a laptop or a tablet, and how the order was placed. The data is summarized in the table below.\n\n|  | In-store | Online | Phone |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Laptops | $35$ | $20$ | $12$ |\n| Tablets | $21$ | $14$ | $x$ |\n\nIf a person who bought a tablet is selected at random, the probability that the person ordered by online is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$. What is the value of $x$?" "An electronics shop records whether a buyer’s main purchase was a laptop or a tablet, and how the order was placed. The data is summarized in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">In-store</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Online</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Phone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Laptops</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Tablets</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf a person who bought a tablet is selected at random, the probability that the person ordered by online is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$. What is the value of $x$?"] [:db/cas 17592186228764 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $x$, we use the given conditional probability: the probability that a person ordered by online, given they bought a tablet, is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nThe conditional probability is calculated as the ratio of the number of people who ordered by phone and bought a tablet to the total number of people who bought a tablet. This is given by\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Online} \\mid \\text{Tablet}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of tablet buyers who ordered by online}}{\\text{Total number who bought a tablet}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that there are\n\n$$\n21 + 14 + x = {\\color{red}35 + x}\n$$\n\npeople who bought a tablet. Of these, ${\\color{blue}14}$ buyers ordered online.\n\n|  | In-store | Online | Phone |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Laptops | $35$ | $20$ | $12$ |\n| Tablets | $21$ | $\\color{blue}14$ | $x$ |\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Online} \\mid \\text{Tablet}) = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}14}{\\color{red}35 + x}\n$$\n\nSince this must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{14}{35 + x} &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{35 + x}{14} &= \\frac{6}{1} \\\\\n35 + x &= 84 \\\\\nx &= 49\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the value of $x$, we use the given conditional probability: the probability that a person ordered by online, given they bought a tablet, is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nThe conditional probability is calculated as the ratio of the number of people who ordered by phone and bought a tablet to the total number of people who bought a tablet. This is given by\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Online} \\mid \\text{Tablet}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of tablet buyers who ordered by online}}{\\text{Total number who bought a tablet}}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that there are\n\n$$\n21 + 14 + x = {\\color{red}35 + x}\n$$\n\npeople who bought a tablet. Of these, ${\\color{blue}14}$ buyers ordered online.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">In-store</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Online</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Phone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Laptops</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$35$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Tablets</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FFF8E5\">$\\color{blue}14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability is\n\n$$\nP(\\text{Online} \\mid \\text{Tablet}) = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}14}{\\color{red}35 + x}\n$$\n\nSince this must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{14}{35 + x} &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{35 + x}{14} &= \\frac{6}{1} \\\\\n35 + x &= 84 \\\\\nx &= 49\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228765 :question/problem "A group of $30$ students, during each of $3$ different practice exams, answered $5$ questions. Each correct response was worth $1$ point. The table shows how many students earned each possible score on each exam.\n\n| Number of Students by Score and Exam | $5$ out of $5$ | $4$ out of $5$ | $3$ out of $5$ | $2$ out of $5$ | $1$ out of $5$ | $0$ out of $5$ | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Exam $1$ | $6$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| **Total** | **$12$** | **$14$** | **$15$** | **$14$** | **$18$** | **$17$** | **$90$** |\n\nNo student received the same score on two different exams. If a student is chosen at random, what is the probability that the student earned a perfect score on Exam $1$ or Exam $3$, given that the student earned a perfect score on one of the three exams?" "A group of $30$ students, during each of $3$ different practice exams, answered $5$ questions. Each correct response was worth $1$ point. The table shows how many students earned each possible score on each exam.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Students by Score and Exam</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$5$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$4$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$3$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Total</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Exam $1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Exam $2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Exam $3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$12$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$14$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$15$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$14$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$18$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$17$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$90$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNo student received the same score on two different exams. If a student is chosen at random, what is the probability that the student earned a perfect score on Exam $1$ or Exam $3$, given that the student earned a perfect score on one of the three exams?"] [:db/cas 17592186228765 :question/worked-solution "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of students who received a score of $5$ on Exam $1$ or Exam $3$ to the total number of students who received a score of $5$ on one of the three exams. This is given by\n$1$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of ${\\color{red}12}$ students received a score of $5$. Of this amount,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6} + {\\color{purple}4} = 10\n$$\n\nstudents received that score in either Exam $1$ or Exam $3$.\n\n| Number of Students by Score and Exam | $5$ out of $5$ | $4$ out of $5$ | $3$ out of $5$ | $2$ out of $5$ | $1$ out of $5$ | $0$ out of $5$ | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Exam $1$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $4$ | $7$ | $5$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| Exam $3$ | $\\color{purple}4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ | $30$ |\n| **Total** | ${\\color{red}12}$ | **$14$** | **$15$** | **$14$** | **$18$** | **$17$** | **$90$** |\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Exam1or3∣Perfect Score) &= \\frac{6 + 4}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To determine the desired probability, we must find the ratio of the number of students who received a score of $5$ on Exam $1$ or Exam $3$ to the total number of students who received a score of $5$ on one of the three exams. This is given by\n$1$\n\nFrom the table, we can see that a total of ${\\color{red}12}$ students received a score of $5$. Of this amount,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6} + {\\color{purple}4} = 10\n$$\n\nstudents received that score in either Exam $1$ or Exam $3$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Number of Students by Score and Exam</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$5$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$4$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$3$ out of $5$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=