/td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nData set $A$ and data set $B$ each contain $27$ values. The table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set. Which of the following statements best compares the means of the two data sets? Round to two decimal places where necessary.\n\n1. The mean of the data set $A$ is less than the mean of the data set $B$.\n2. The mean of the data set $A$ is equal to the mean of the data set $B$.\n3. The mean of the data set $A$ is greater than the mean of the data set $B$.\n4. There is not enough information to compare the means of the two datasets."] [:db/cas 17592186228774 :question/problem "| Museum | $2012$ | $2013$ | $2014$ | $2015$ | $2016$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| North Museum | $540$ | $460$ | $520$ | $500$ | $480$ |\n| South Museum | $300$ | $480$ | $500$ | $510$ | $520$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of visitors at two museums in each of the five years. No visitor was counted at both museums.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the number of visitors over the $5$ years shown?\n\n1. The mean number from North Museum is greater than the mean number from South Museum.\n2. The median number from North Museum is greater than the median number from South Museum." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Museum</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">$2012$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">$2013$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">$2014$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">$2015$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">$2016$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">North Museum</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$540$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$460$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$520$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$500$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$480$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">South Museum</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$300$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$480$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$500$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$510$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$520$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table above shows the number of visitors at two museums in each of the five years. No visitor was counted at both museums.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the number of visitors over the $5$ years shown?\n\n1. The mean number from North Museum is greater than the mean number from South Museum.\n2. The median number from North Museum is greater than the median number from South Museum."] [:db/cas 17592186228801 :question/problem "Veterinarians sometimes estimate a dog's age in years by multiplying its weight in pounds by a constant growth factor that varies depending on the breed. The table below shows the growth factors for several dog breeds.\n\n| Dog Breed | Growth<br>factor |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Labrador Retriever | $5$ |\n| German Shepherd | $5.5$ |\n| Golden Retriever | $5$ |\n| Bulldog | $5$ |\n| Beagle | $4$ |\n| Boxer | $5.5$ |\n| Poodle | $4.5$ |\n| Rottweiler | $6$ |\n\nSuppose a Golden Retriever and a Poodle each currently weigh $14$ pounds. Which of the following is closest to the difference, in pounds, between their weights $11$ years from now?" "Veterinarians sometimes estimate a dog's age in years by multiplying its weight in pounds by a constant growth factor that varies depending on the breed. The table below shows the growth factors for several dog breeds.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Dog Breed</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Growth<br>factor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Labrador Retriever</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">German Shepherd</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Golden Retriever</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Bulldog</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Beagle</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Boxer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Poodle</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Rottweiler</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose a Golden Retriever and a Poodle each currently weigh $14$ pounds. Which of the following is closest to the difference, in pounds, between their weights $11$ years from now?"] [:db/cas 17592186228828 :question/problem "Two towns grow as summarized in the table. The population of City A grows by a percentage each year.\n\n|  | Initial population | Yearly increase |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| City A | $50{,}000$ | $3\\%$ per year (applied to current population) |\n| City B | $55{,}000$ | $1{,}200$ people per year |\n\nWhich of the following is true about the populations?\n\n1. City A always gains more people per year than City B.\n2. City A gains more people per year than City B at first, but eventually gains less people per year.\n3. City A always gains less people per year than City B.\n4. City A gains less people per year than City B at first, but eventually gains more people per year." "Two towns grow as summarized in the table. The population of City A grows by a percentage each year.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Initial population</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Yearly increase</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">City A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$50{,}000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\%$ per year (applied to current population)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">City B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$55{,}000$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1{,}200$ people per year</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following is true about the populations?\n\n1. City A always gains more people per year than City B.\n2. City A gains more people per year than City B at first, but eventually gains less people per year.\n3. City A always gains less people per year than City B.\n4. City A gains less people per year than City B at first, but eventually gains more people per year."] [:db/cas 17592186228829 :question/problem "The table shows selected values from the function $k$.\n\n| $x$ | $k(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |\n| $2$ | $-9$ |\n\nWhich of the following is the best description of function $k$?\n\n1. Decreasing exponential\n2. Increasing exponential\n3. Increasing linear\n4. Decreasing linear" "The table shows selected values from the function $k$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k(x)$</th></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-9$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following is the best description of function $k$?\n\n1. Decreasing exponential\n2. Increasing exponential\n3. Increasing linear\n4. Decreasing linear"] [:db/cas 17592186228841 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram with groups, the horizontal axis shows the value groups, and the vertical axis tells us how many values fall into each group.\n\nIn our case, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $15-18$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $18-21$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n| $21-24$ | $3$ | $15$ |\n| $24-27$ | $2$ | $17$ |\n| $27-30$ | $2$ | $19$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values is\n\n$$\n7+5+3+2+2=19\n$$\n\nSince this is an odd number, the median is the value in position\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{19+1}{2}=\\dfrac{20}{2}=10\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $10$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the first row, the table covers $7$ positions in total.\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $10$th value falls in the second interval, which is\n$18 - 21$\nas shown below.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $15-18$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $18-21$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n| $21-24$ | $3$ | $15$ |\n| $24-27$ | $2$ | $17$ |\n| $27-30$ | $2$ | $19$ |\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of the median is $18$." "To estimate the median from a histogram, we first convert the histogram into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram with groups, the horizontal axis shows the value groups, and the vertical axis tells us how many values fall into each group.\n\nIn our case, we build the frequency table below and compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18-21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21-24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24-27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27-30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values is\n\n$$\n7+5+3+2+2=19\n$$\n\nSince this is an odd number, the median is the value in position\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{19+1}{2}=\\dfrac{20}{2}=10\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $10$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the first row, the table covers $7$ positions in total.\n- Up to the second row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $10$th value falls in the second interval, which is\n$18 - 21$\nas shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$18-21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21-24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24-27$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27-30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of the median is $18$."] [:db/cas 17592186228842 :question/problem "| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $22-25$ | $4$ |\n| $25-28$ | $7$ |\n| $28-31$ | $2$ |\n| $31-34$ | $5$ |\n| $34-37$ | $3$ |\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the largest possible value of the mean?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22-25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25-28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28-31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31-34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$34-37$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the largest possible value of the mean?"] [:db/cas 17592186228842 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the mean from grouped data, we first use the frequency table.\n\nSince we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the largest possible value of $x$ for each group.\n\nThen, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.\n\n| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ | $x_{\\textrm{max}}$ | $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $22-25$ | $4$ | $25$ | $4\\cdot 25 = 100$ |\n| $25-28$ | $7$ | $28$ | $7\\cdot 28 = 196$ |\n| $28-31$ | $2$ | $31$ | $2\\cdot 31 = 62$ |\n| $31-34$ | $5$ | $34$ | $5\\cdot 34 = 170$ |\n| $34-37$ | $3$ | $37$ | $3\\cdot 37 = 111$ |\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlargest possible mean &= \\frac{100 + 196 + 62 + 170 + 111}{4 + 7 + 2 + 5 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{639}{21} \\\\\n&\\approx 30.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "To estimate the mean from grouped data, we first use the frequency table.\n\nSince we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the largest possible value of $x$ for each group.\n\nThen, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{max}}$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22-25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4\\cdot 25 = 100$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25-28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7\\cdot 28 = 196$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28-31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2\\cdot 31 = 62$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31-34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5\\cdot 34 = 170$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$34-37$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$37$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\cdot 37 = 111$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlargest possible mean &= \\frac{100 + 196 + 62 + 170 + 111}{4 + 7 + 2 + 5 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{639}{21} \\\\\n&\\approx 30.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186228843 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the smallest possible range, we take the smallest possible maximum value and subtract the largest possible minimum value.\n\nLet's start by turning our histogram into a frequency table.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $30-\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{35}$ | $6$ |\n| $36-37$ | $8$ |\n| $38-40$ | $10$ |\n| $41-43$ | $7$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{44}-51$ | $4$ |\n\nLooking at the table,\n\n- the smallest possible maximum value is $44$, and\n- the largest possible minimum value is $35$.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible range is\n\n$$\n44 - 35 = 9\n$$" "To calculate the smallest possible range, we take the smallest possible maximum value and subtract the largest possible minimum value.\n\nLet's start by turning our histogram into a frequency table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30-\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{35}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36-37$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$38-40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$41-43$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{44}-51$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLooking at the table,\n\n- the smallest possible maximum value is $44$, and\n- the largest possible minimum value is $35$.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible range is\n\n$$\n44 - 35 = 9\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228845 :question/problem "| **Ivy** | $3.6$ | $3.8$ | $4.0$ | $3.9$ | $4.2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Jack** | $4.4$ | $4.2$ | $4.0$ | $3.8$ | $x$ |\n\nIvy and Jack each attempted the math test five times, and their scores are represented in the table above. The mean score for Jack’s attempts was $0.2$ greater than the mean score for Ivy’s attempts. What is the value of $x$?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ivy</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Jack</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIvy and Jack each attempted the math test five times, and their scores are represented in the table above. The mean score for Jack’s attempts was $0.2$ greater than the mean score for Ivy’s attempts. What is the value of $x$?"] [:db/cas 17592186228855 :question/problem "A local survey polled $1,300$ voters about whether they support a new bond measure. The table below summarizes all $1,300$ responses.\n\n|  | Under $40$ | $40$ and Over |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Support | $370$ | $380$ |\n| Do Not Support | ? | $300$ |\n\nHow many of the voters under $40$ surveyed responded that they do not support the bond measure?" "A local survey polled $1,300$ voters about whether they support a new bond measure. The table below summarizes all $1,300$ responses.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Under $40$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$40$ and Over</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Support</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$370$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$380$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"left\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Do Not Support</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">?</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$300$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHow many of the voters under $40$ surveyed responded that they do not support the bond measure?"] [:db/cas 17592186228864 :question/problem "A table shows the average annual rainfall in a region for six different years.\n\n| Year | Average rainfall (cm) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1985$ | $100$ |\n| $1995$ | $120$ |\n| $2005$ | $150$ |\n| $2010$ | $180$ |\n| $2015$ | $200$ |\n| $2025$ | $220$ |\n\nWhat was the percentage increase in rainfall from $1995$ to $2010$?" "A table shows the average annual rainfall in a region for six different years.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Year</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Average rainfall (cm)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1985$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1995$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$120$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2005$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$150$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2010$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$180$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2015$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$200$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2025$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$220$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat was the percentage increase in rainfall from $1995$ to $2010$?"] [:db/cas 17592186228865 :question/problem "A total of $34,\\!852$ students study at a certain university. The university conducted a survey among a randomly chosen sample of $1,\\!200$ students about their preference for morning or evening classes. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n|  | Morning | Evening | No Preference | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Freshmen and Sophomores | $280$ | $190$ | $30$ | $500$ |\n| Juniors and Seniors | $210$ | $150$ | $40$ | $400$ |\n| Graduates | $120$ | $100$ | $80$ | $300$ |\n| Total | $610$ | $440$ | $150$ | $1,\\!200$ |\n\nWhat is the difference between the percentage of undergraduates (freshmen, sophomores, juniors, and seniors combined) who preferred morning classes and the percentage of graduate students who preferred morning classes? Rounded to the nearest whole number." "A total of $34,\\!852$ students study at a certain university. The university conducted a survey among a randomly chosen sample of $1,\\!200$ students about their preference for morning or evening classes. The results are shown in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Morning</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Evening</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">No Preference</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Total</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Freshmen and Sophomores</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$280$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$190$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$500$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Juniors and Seniors</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$210$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$150$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$400$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Graduates</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$120$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$300$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$610$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$440$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$150$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1,\\!200$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhat is the difference between the percentage of undergraduates (freshmen, sophomores, juniors, and seniors combined) who preferred morning classes and the percentage of graduate students who preferred morning classes? Rounded to the nearest whole number."] [:db/cas 17592186228866 :question/problem "A hospital tracked the number of patient visits in two consecutive years, as shown below.\n\n| Year | Patient visits |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2020$ | $30,\\!000$ |\n| $2021$ | $31,\\!500$ |\n\nThe hospital administrator estimated that the percentage increase from $2021$ to $2022$ would be four times the percentage increase from $2020$ to $2021$. How many visits did the administrator expect in $2022$?" "A hospital tracked the number of patient visits in two consecutive years, as shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Year</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Patient visits</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2020$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30,\\!000$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2021$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31,\\!500$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe hospital administrator estimated that the percentage increase from $2021$ to $2022$ would be four times the percentage increase from $2020$ to $2021$. How many visits did the administrator expect in $2022$?"] [:db/cas 17592186229285 :question/problem "| Response | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Every day | $5$ |\n| Several times a week | $12$ |\n| Once a week | $22$ |\n| A few times a month | $58$ |\n| Rarely | $47$ |\n| Never | $36$ |\n| Total | $180$ |\n\nThe table above shows results from a survey of $180$ students who were asked how often they exercise outdoors. How many students answered either \"rarely\" or \"never\"?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Response</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">Every day</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">Several times a week</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">Once a week</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">A few times a month</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$58$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">Rarely</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$47$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">Never</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$180$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table above shows results from a survey of $180$ students who were asked how often they exercise outdoors. How many students answered either \"rarely\" or \"never\"?"] [:db/cas 17592186229286 :question/problem "| Type of facility | Average number of staff |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Hospital | $420$ |\n| Clinic | $95$ |\n| Urgent care center | $145$ |\n\nBased on the table above, how many more staff members does a hospital have on average compared to a clinic?" "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Type of facility</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\">Average number of staff</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Hospital</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$420$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Clinic</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$95$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Urgent care center</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$145$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nBased on the table above, how many more staff members does a hospital have on average compared to a clinic?"] [:db/cas 17592186229302 :question/problem "| Data value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $5$ |\n| $6$ | $7$ |\n| $7$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $10$ |\n| $9$ | $8$ |\n| $10$ | $5$ |\n| $11$ | $0$ |\n| $12$ 