${min}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-4}}"] [:db/cas 17592186231770 :question/problem "A bank has created a list of customers with various balances in their bank accounts. The data is shown below.\n\n| Customer | Bank Balance |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Mr. Jones | $-\\$329$ |\n| Ms. Chin | $\\$16$ |\n| Ms. Hernandez | $\\$1256$ |\n| Mr. Pulev | $-\\$67$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Mr. Jones owes $\\$329$ to the bank.\n2. Ms. Chin has the lowest bank balance.\n3. Mr. Pulev owes less money to the bank than Ms. Chin." "A bank has created a list of customers with various balances in their bank accounts. The data is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Customer</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Bank Balance</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Mr. Jones</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\$329$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Ms. Chin</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Ms. Hernandez</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\$1256$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Mr. Pulev</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\$67$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Mr. Jones owes $\\$329$ to the bank.\n2. Ms. Chin has the lowest bank balance.\n3. Mr. Pulev owes less money to the bank than Ms. Chin."] [:db/cas 17592186231786 :question/problem "Four people are walking along a well-known trekking route. The table below shows each person's position, represented as the distance walked since passing a resting area.\n\n| Person | Distance (m) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Spencer | $400$ |\n| Emelia | $-150$ |\n| Isadore | $600$ |\n| Suz | $-800$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Spencer is $400m$ before reaching the resting area.\n2. Isadore has walked farther past the resting area than Spencer.\n3. Emelia is closer to the resting area than Suz." "Four people are walking along a well-known trekking route. The table below shows each person's position, represented as the distance walked since passing a resting area.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Person</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Distance (m)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Spencer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$400$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Emelia</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-150$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Isadore</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$600$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Suz</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-800$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Spencer is $400m$ before reaching the resting area.\n2. Isadore has walked farther past the resting area than Spencer.\n3. Emelia is closer to the resting area than Suz."] [:db/cas 17592186232033 :tutorial/content "Let's consider another example of interpreting the absolute value in context.\n\nSuppose an outdoor swimming pool opens only if the average temperature over the day is above $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}$. The table below shows the daily average temperatures above $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}$ during a week.\n\n| Day | Degrees above min. opening temp |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Monday | $-2$ |\n| Tuesday | $4$ |\n| Wednesday | $1$ |\n| Thursday | $-3$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Monday's average temperature was $2$ degrees more than the minimum required for the pool to open.\nII. Thursday had the lowest average temperature.\nIII. Tuesday's average temperature was greater than Wednesday's average temperature.\n\nIn this context, a negative number represents a deficit, and the absolute value of the deficit gives the number of degrees the temperature is below the minimum for the pool to open.\n\nLet's go through each statement:\n\n- Monday has a negative value, and so the average temperature has a deficit for the pool to open. The number of degrees the temperature is below the minimum for the pool to open is $\\mid -2 \\mid = 2$. Hence, statement I is false.\n- The day with the lowest average temperature is the day whose temperature is most to the left on a number line.Clearly, Thursday's average temperature is most to the left, and so it had the lowest temperature. Hence, statement II is true.\n- Tuesday's average temperature is $4$, while Wednesday's is $1$. Since $4 > 1$, Tuesday's average temperature was greater than Wednesday's average temperature. Hence, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "Let's consider another example of interpreting the absolute value in context.\n\nSuppose an outdoor swimming pool opens only if the average temperature over the day is above $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}$. The table below shows the daily average temperatures above $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}$ during a week.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Day</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Degrees above min. opening temp</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Monday</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr 